# Data Dawg — athlete summary on top

For **any Data Dawg answer about a specific athlete** (search, “who is…”, “tell me about…”, dossier):

1. **Top of answer** = linked name via `Here's what I found about [Name](profile_url):` then an **analyst lead paragraph** (dossier uses `formatAnalystAthleteOpening` / `buildAnalystLeadParagraph` in `lib/data-dawg-athlete-analyst-profile.ts`; lighter surfaces may still use `formatAthleteAnswerOpening` in `lib/athlete-profile-links.ts`).
2. **Name is the profile hyperlink** — Use `getAthleteProfileUrl(id)` → `https://app.ncwrestlingunited.com/view-profile?id=…` (not `/unified-profile/[id]` and not `/athletes/[id]`). In chat HTML, open those links **same-tab** (never `target="_blank"` on app profile routes).
3. Include **career record**, **High School**, **Class of**, and **College commit** (with division when known) early — dossier: Career snapshot under the lead. **Do not** put **Weight** in the header — it changes often; weight belongs on tournament result lines only.
4. Prefer **compact Hall-of-Fame storytelling** (avoid repeating the same facts): Opening → Career snapshot → Development path (HS / club / **NC United** Blue + National Team) → Career progression → Historical context (one paragraph) → **Detailed results** → verified sources. Do **not** emit Notable achievements, Represented North Carolina (fold into Development path), a closing coda, or a rankings dump that restates the snapshot. Four straight titles = **four consecutive** / every high school season — never “back-to-back.” Four-time group membership is “one of N” (label chronological order clearly if mentioned). Super32 “Best listed result” uses best W–L/placement. Never infer Fargo “Blood Round” from W–L unless the round is explicitly stored.
5. **No markdown headings** (`###` / `##` / `#`). Plain section labels only (e.g. `Career progression:`, `Historical context:`). Postprocess strips leading `#` markers.
6. Do not invent a profile URL without a directory id; tournament-only alumni can use a plain name. Historical context must be **derived from verified data** — never invent school prestige without dual-title evidence. College transfers: snapshot `UNC → NC State` (original first). Alumni use “finished his … career”; current Class of athletes (not yet past June of grad year) use present tense (“is a … wrestler who is … / He is W–L …”). Never say a Class of 2027 finished his HS career in 2026.
